From 0ffc59d9b95596e7cfd10514718de638ddfaec85 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "shubham.paralikar" <shubham.paralikar@mediatek.com>
Date: Wed, 8 Jul 2026 15:19:19 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] NVIDIA: SAUCE: pinctrl: mediatek: Add bus-hold bias and
 program-by-GPIO helper

BugLink: https://bugs.launchpad.net/bugs/2167299

The MediaTek pinctrl bias code only knows pull-up and pull-down. The
pad "bus keeper" mode, with both PU and PD enabled, cannot be
requested or read back, yet the MT8901 SoundWire pads need it: the
ACPI _CRS for the SoundWire controller carries PinFunction() vendor
encodings that ask for bus-keeper on the data lines, and without it
codec enumeration fails unless the pad registers are written by hand.

Add MTK_BUS_HOLD as a third pull mode in the PU/PD bias path and wire
PIN_CONFIG_BIAS_BUS_HOLD through the pinctrl-paris get/set handlers.
The generic bias-bus-hold property carries no argument, so the set
handler always requests MTK_ENABLE for it. Bus-hold is only
expressible through the PU/PD bits. The PULLSEL/PULLEN and R1R0 bias
paths reject it, and on pins that also have RSEL the RSEL step is
skipped so the request is served by PU/PD and the existing RSEL value
is retained. The bias getters report PU=1, PD=1 back as MTK_BUS_HOLD.

Register every mtk_pinctrl instance in a global list at probe and
export mtk_pinctrl_program_bias_by_gpio() through
<linux/soc/mediatek/mtk-pinctrl.h>, so a client driver that parses
PinFunction() vendor encodings from its own _CRS can apply pad bias
by absolute SoC GPIO number without going through pinctrl states.
The instance is unregistered via a devm action so it leaves the list
before devm frees it, and it is registered before the gpiochip is
added so that a failure to install that action has nothing non-devm
to unwind. MediaTek pin numbers are zero-based per controller, so the
helper refuses with -EINVAL if more than one registered controller
could own the requested pin instead of programming whichever probed
first. Every supported platform registers a single pinctrl instance,
so this cannot trigger today. The helper returns -EPROBE_DEFER while
no controller has registered, so a consumer that probes before
pinctrl is retried by the driver core instead of treating the missing
controller as a firmware error.

The helper bypasses the pinctrl core and therefore the core's
per-controller mutex that serialises normal pinconf operations. The
PU/PD setter writes the two bits as separate register updates, so a
helper call could interleave with a concurrent mtk_pinconf_set() on
the same pin and leave the pad in a state neither caller asked for
while both report success. Add a per-controller pinconf_lock mutex
taken by mtk_pinconf_set(), mtk_pinconf_get() and the helper so the
two paths exclude each other.

Signed-off-by: shubham.paralikar <shubham.paralikar@mediatek.com>
Signed-off-by: Kiran Maddaraki <kmaddaraki@nvidia.com>
Acked-by: Jamie Nguyen <jamien@nvidia.com>
Acked-by: Carol L Soto <csoto@nvidia.com>
Signed-off-by: Brad Figg <bfigg@nvidia.com>
---
